\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{The limit retains the second-order coefficient}
\date{2026-11-06}

\p{A finite collection of the Fourier moments in
\ref{fdip-00CC}, or of the block means in \ref{fdip-00CD}, is
a centered Gaussian vector. In an orthonormal eigenchart the
[Gaussian probability law](fdip-0005) is a product of centered
scalar Gaussians, and the scalar law of variance #{v} has
characteristic function #{\exp(-\frac{1}{2}vt^2)}, as used in
\ref{fdip-00C1}. With \ref{fdip-000B}, the vector
characteristic function is #{\exp(-\frac{1}{2}\zeta\cdot C\zeta)}.
Convergence of the covariance #{C} makes these functions
converge to a continuous characteristic function, which
determines the limiting law. When #{R=1} and #{c_1=1}, one has #{\kappa=1}. The Fourier
moment is then the moment of \ref{fdip-004J}, and the block
matrix is the matrix of \ref{fdip-00BO}.}

\p{The argument gives no speed of convergence. It concerns
these coordinates and these means. Removal of sites is not
treated.}
